1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,742
Claude can write code and answer questions. But it can't see your orders, your database, or your own tools.

2
00:00:07,192 --> 00:00:14,680
MCP fixes that. In this video, you'll learn what an MCP server and an MCP client are, and when to build each one.

3
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:21,109
Then we'll build a real MCP server and a real client in Python, and connect the server to Claude Code.

4
00:00:22,659 --> 00:00:23,762
So, what is MCP?

5
00:00:24,212 --> 00:00:37,678
MCP, the Model Context Protocol, is an open standard that connects AI apps to data and tools. The official docs compare it to a USB-C port for AI apps: one standard plug, for everything.

6
00:00:38,128 --> 00:00:44,289
An MCP server is a program that offers things to the AI: tools it can call, and data it can read.

7
00:00:44,739 --> 00:00:53,169
An MCP client is the connector inside the AI app. The app, called the host, creates one client for each server it connects to.

8
00:00:54,619 --> 00:00:56,114
Here's how the pieces fit together.

9
00:00:56,564 --> 00:01:01,730
The host is the AI app you use, like Claude Code, Claude Desktop, or VS Code.

10
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:12,532
A server offers three kinds of things: tools, which are actions the AI can call; resources, which is read-only data; and prompts, which are ready-made templates.

11
00:01:12,982 --> 00:01:24,286
They talk over a transport. With stdio, the client starts the server as a program on your own machine. With Streamable HTTP, the server runs remotely, on the internet.

12
00:01:24,736 --> 00:01:30,135
And that's the big idea: you build a server once, and every app that speaks MCP can use it.

13
00:01:31,585 --> 00:01:32,633
So when do you build what?

14
00:01:33,083 --> 00:01:44,556
Build an MCP server when your data or your tool should work in many AI apps: Claude Code, Claude Desktop, VS Code, and others, without writing one integration for each.

15
00:01:45,006 --> 00:01:51,500
Build an MCP client when you're making your own AI app or agent, and you want it to use servers that already exist.

16
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:58,703
If a server already exists, for GitHub, Notion or Sentry for example, you don't build anything. You just add it.

17
00:01:59,153 --> 00:02:06,848
And if it's one script, in one app, with one or two tools, skip MCP. Plain tool use in the Claude API is simpler.

18
00:02:08,398 --> 00:02:15,380
Our use case: Maple Mugs, the small online shop from my earlier videos. Customers keep asking: where is my order?

19
00:02:15,830 --> 00:02:22,967
Without MCP, Claude can't reach the orders database. You look up the order yourself, and copy-paste it. Every time.

20
00:02:23,417 --> 00:02:32,111
With an MCP server, Claude gets a get order tool. It calls it, and answers directly. In Claude Code, or in any app that speaks MCP.

21
00:02:33,561 --> 00:02:39,789
Here are our orders: three of them, each with a customer, an item, a status and a delivery date.

22
00:02:40,239 --> 00:02:49,379
In a real shop, orders live in a database or an online store system, not in a file next to your code. Here, this small JSON file plays that role.

23
00:02:49,829 --> 00:02:59,565
Claude Code, Claude Desktop and VS Code can't reach that database on their own. And writing a separate integration for each app means doing the same work three times.

24
00:03:00,215 --> 00:03:04,815
So let's wrap the order lookup in one MCP server, and every app can use it.

25
00:03:06,165 --> 00:03:07,907
Step one: build the server.

26
00:03:08,357 --> 00:03:17,544
First, install the MCP Python SDK with pip install mcp. Then I create an MCP server, and give it a name: maple mugs.

27
00:03:20,324 --> 00:03:32,033
The tool decorator turns a normal Python function into an MCP tool. The type hint and the docstring become the tool's description and input schema. That's what Claude reads, to decide when to call it.

28
00:03:32,483 --> 00:03:37,368
Then a resource: read-only data, here the shipping policy, with its own address.

29
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:46,045
And at the bottom, run over stdio. The client will start this file as a program, and talk to it through its input and output.

30
00:03:46,895 --> 00:03:48,517
Step two: build a client.

31
00:03:48,967 --> 00:03:55,597
A client is what an AI app uses to talk to a server. Writing a tiny one is also the best way to test your server.

32
00:03:59,061 --> 00:04:04,518
First, how to start the server: a command and its arguments, just like typing it in a terminal.

33
00:04:04,968 --> 00:04:12,569
Then, async with Client. Entering this block launches the server and agrees on the protocol. Leaving it shuts the server down.

34
00:04:13,019 --> 00:04:18,369
Inside, I list the server's tools, and call get order by name, with its arguments.

35
00:04:19,219 --> 00:04:20,516
Step three: run it.

36
00:04:20,966 --> 00:04:22,716
Let's look up order 1042.

37
00:04:23,466 --> 00:04:31,309
The client started the server, found one tool, get order, and called it. Here's the order: shipped, arriving October twenty-fifth.

38
00:04:31,759 --> 00:04:34,285
And an order that doesn't exist gets a clear message.

39
00:04:34,735 --> 00:04:43,736
No API key needed so far. MCP is just a protocol between two programs. The files include tests that run this same server and client.

40
00:04:44,686 --> 00:04:46,687
Step four: connect Claude Code.

41
00:04:47,137 --> 00:04:54,857
One command: claude mcp add, the stdio transport, a name, then two dashes, and the command that starts the server.

42
00:04:55,307 --> 00:05:03,024
Everything after the two dashes is passed to the server, untouched. Now Claude Code is the host, and it creates a client for our server.

43
00:05:03,474 --> 00:05:06,132
Now let's ask Claude where order 1042 is.

44
00:05:06,582 --> 00:05:11,988
Claude calls the get order tool from our server. The first time, Claude Code asks for your permission.

45
00:05:12,438 --> 00:05:18,057
And there's the answer, straight from our order data. Same server, now used by a real AI app.

46
00:05:19,607 --> 00:05:20,195
Four tips.

47
00:05:20,645 --> 00:05:29,683
One: in a stdio server, never use print. Standard output carries the protocol messages, and a print breaks them. Use the logging module instead.

48
00:05:30,133 --> 00:05:35,978
Two: write clear docstrings. They're what Claude reads, to decide which tool to call, and when.

49
00:05:36,428 --> 00:05:43,554
Three: test your server with a small client, like ours, or with the MCP Inspector, before you connect it to an app.

50
00:05:44,004 --> 00:05:50,709
And four: only connect servers you trust. A server that fetches outside content can expose you to prompt injection.

51
00:05:52,259 --> 00:05:52,913
Let's recap.

52
00:05:53,363 --> 00:06:00,060
An MCP server offers tools and data. An MCP client, inside the AI app, connects to it.

53
00:06:00,510 --> 00:06:07,304
Build a server when your data should work in many AI apps. Build a client when your own app should use existing servers.

54
00:06:07,754 --> 00:06:12,178
And to use a server in Claude Code, it's one command: claude mcp add.

55
00:06:13,628 --> 00:06:20,346
The server, the client, a cheat sheet, and the tests are linked in the description. They run with no API key.

56
00:06:20,796 --> 00:06:27,627
If this helped, like the video, and subscribe to Zero Cost AI for more free AI tutorials. See you in the next one!

